~ Concurrency runtime — the `@sleep` leaf IO primitive (see docs/concurrency/README.md).
~ `@sleep(seconds)` is an effect-only pause: used as a statement it waits right there on
~ the current fiber, then execution continues in program order. It carries no value yet —
~ the deferred-value / overlap story arrives with a value-returning primitive (`@readStdin`).
~ `time.now()` reads a monotonic clock, so this example VERIFIES the pause actually waited: the
~ elapsed time across the sleep is at least the requested duration. Self-asserting via
~ core.test; exit 0 = pass.

<< core.test
<< core.time

~ The clock is reached only as `time.now()`, so a program's own bare `now` is an
~ unrelated function — here one that scales the clock (seconds to milliseconds, say).
now = (scale :: Num) -> Num => time.now() * scale

^ = () -> Num => <
  start = time.now()
  @sleep(0.05)                    ~ pause ~50ms
  ~ A sleep waits AT LEAST its duration, so this bound is deterministic (never flaky).
  assert(time.now() - start >= 0.05, equals(true))
  assert(now(1000) - start * 1000 >= 50, equals(true))   ~ the same bound in milliseconds

  ~ Ordinary code runs after the pause, computing a ready value.
  answer = 6 * 7
  assert(answer, equals(42))

  0
>
